 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T20:24+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes—given your asthma, I’d stay indoors as much as practical in Kuala Lumpur today, especially avoiding exercise or prolonged time outside.** The latest readings I found were elevated: about **164 on Malaysia’s API scale** at a KL-area station around 4 p.m., and another source reported a U.S.-scale AQI of **181**. Readings vary by station and scale, but both indicate poor air for someone with asthma. ([jerebumalaysia.com](https://jerebumalaysia.com/en/stations/batu-muda-w-p-kuala-lumpur/?utm_source=openai))\n\nKeep windows closed if the air seems hazy, use a HEPA air purifier if you have one, and keep your reliever inhaler and asthma action plan handy. Malaysia’s guidance recommends limiting outdoor activity for at-risk people when API is 101–200; AirNow also advises people with lung disease to reduce exposure when air quality is unhealthy. ([eqms.doe.gov.my](https://eqms.doe.gov.my/Documents/APIMS/API_Calculation.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you must go out, keep it brief and easy, and check your nearby reading on Malaysia’s APIMS first. If breathing becomes seriously difficult or your usual reliever isn’t helping, seek urgent medical care.",
